<h2 id="section-pairs">Pairs that examiners love</h2>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>its / it’s</b> — its = belonging; it’s = it is / it has.</p>
<p><b>whose / who’s</b> — whose = belonging; who’s = who is / who has.</p>
<p><b>defining / non-defining</b> — essential information = no commas; extra information = commas.</p>
<p><b>comma splice / semicolon</b> — two independent clauses joined only by a comma is wrong; use semicolon or full stop.</p>
<p><b>direct / reported question</b> — direct takes ? ; reported takes full stop.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="section-mistakes">8 high-frequency mistakes in exams</h2>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>1. Extra comma after long subject.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ The students who cleared the cut-off, were called for document verification.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ … cut-off were called …</span></p>
<p><b>2. Missing apostrophe in plural ownership.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ The teachers room is on the first floor.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ The teachers’ room …</span></p>
<p><b>3. It’s vs its.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ The committee has submitted it’s report.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ … its report.</span></p>
<p><b>4. Comma splice.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ The paper was lengthy, many left it incomplete.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ … lengthy; many left …</span></p>
<p><b>5. Question mark on reported speech.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ He asked me where was I going?</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ … where I was going.</span></p>
<p><b>6. Missing closing quotation mark.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ She said, “I have finished the syllabus.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ … syllabus.”</span></p>
<p><b>7. Colon after a verb.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ She wants: a high score and a good rank.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ She wants a high score and a good rank. / She has two aims: a high score and a good rank.</span></p>
<p><b>8. Apostrophe in ordinary plurals.</b> <span class="zp-no">✗ The 1990’s were important.</span> <span class="zp-yes">✓ The 1990s were important.</span></p>
</div>

<h2 id="section-method">The 5-step punctuation check</h2>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>Step 1: Spot every mark and every missing mark.</b> Underline commas, apostrophes, quotes, colons, semicolons and end marks.</p>
<p><b>Step 2: Name the box.</b> Is this a comma issue, an apostrophe issue, a quote issue, etc.?</p>
<p><b>Step 3: Apply the core rule of that box.</b> Defining or non-defining? Ownership or contraction? Exact words or reported speech?</p>
<p><b>Step 4: Check the pairs.</b> its/it’s, whose/who’s, defining/non-defining, comma splice.</p>
<p><b>Step 5: Read the sentence without the mark.</b> If the meaning collapses or becomes ambiguous, the mark is required; if the meaning stays clear and the clause is defining, remove the extra mark.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="section-plan">The 5-day revision plan</h2>
<p>Twenty minutes a day is enough.</p>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>Day 1: Comma rules.</b> Write five defining and five non-defining sentences. Practise removing the forbidden subject-verb comma.</p>
<p><b>Day 2: Apostrophe.</b> Make a two-column list: ownership vs contraction. Write ten its/it’s and whose/who’s sentences.</p>
<p><b>Day 3: Quotes, colon, semicolon.</b> Practise direct speech, lists after colons, and two independent clauses joined by semicolon.</p>
<p><b>Day 4: End marks and reported speech.</b> Convert ten direct questions into reported speech and remove the question mark.</p>
<p><b>Day 5: Mixed practice.</b> Solve the practice set below under timed conditions. Note every error and revisit that box.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="section-practice">Practice set: error spotting</h2>
<p>These are original practice sentences written for this guide.</p>
<h3>Round 1: spot the error</h3>
<p>Each sentence has one punctuation error. Identify and correct it.</p>
<ol>
<li>The candidates who scored the highest marks, were called for the interview.</li>
<li>Its important to revise the notes daily.</li>
<li>She said, “I have completed the syllabus.</li>
<li>The paper was difficult, many students could not finish it.</li>
<li>My brother who lives in Delhi is a doctor.</li>
<li>The teachers room is on the second floor.</li>
<li>He asked me where was I going?</li>
<li>She has three goals a government job, a stable income and respect.</li>
</ol>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>Answers</b></p>
<p>1. Remove the comma after marks (defining clause). &nbsp; 2. It’s important … (contraction). &nbsp; 3. Close the quotation: … syllabus.” &nbsp; 4. Use semicolon or full stop: … difficult; many students … &nbsp; 5. Add commas if non-defining: My brother, who lives in Delhi, is a doctor. &nbsp; 6. teachers’ room (plural ownership). &nbsp; 7. Remove question mark and invert: … where I was going. &nbsp; 8. Add colon: … three goals: a government job …</p>
</div>
<h3>Round 2: fill in the correct mark</h3>
<p>Choose the correct punctuation or write “none”.</p>
<ol>
<li>The results of the examination ___ were declared yesterday. (comma / none)</li>
<li>The dog wagged ___ tail happily. (its / it’s)</li>
<li>“Will you join us tomorrow___” she asked. (? / .)</li>
<li>He studied hard___ therefore he scored well. (; / ,)</li>
<li>Whose / Who’s bag is lying on the bench?</li>
<li>She wants only one thing___ selection in the first attempt. (: / ,)</li>
<li>The 1990___ were a decade of change. (s / ’s)</li>
<li>After submitting the form___ she left the centre. (, / none)</li>
</ol>
<div class="zp-box">
<p><b>Answers</b></p>
<p>1. none &nbsp; 2. its &nbsp; 3. ? &nbsp; 4. ; &nbsp; 5. Whose &nbsp; 6. : &nbsp; 7. s &nbsp; 8. ,</p>
</div>
<p>Scored 7 or more? You are exam-ready on punctuation. Below 5? Revisit Boxes 1 and 2 for two more days.</p>

<h2 id="section-faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is the Oxford comma compulsory in SSC and bank exams?</h3>
<p>No. Both “A, B and C” and “A, B, and C” are accepted provided the paper is consistent. The error usually lies in a missing or extra comma that changes meaning, not in the presence or absence of the final serial comma.</p>
<h3>How many punctuation questions appear in SSC CGL Tier-1?</h3>
<p>Usually 0–2 direct questions, but punctuation errors are frequently embedded inside error-spotting and sentence-improvement questions. Treating them as a separate skill pays off.</p>
<h3>Should I use a semicolon or a full stop between two independent clauses?</h3>
<p>Both are correct. The semicolon shows a closer relationship between the ideas; the full stop is safer if you are unsure.</p>
<h3>Is “it’s” ever used for possession?</h3>
<p>Never. “It’s” is only the contraction of “it is” or “it has.” Possession is always “its” without an apostrophe.</p>
<h3>What is the fastest way to improve?</h3>
<p>Practise the 5-step check on every error-spotting set you solve. Five focused days on the six boxes remove most of the recurring mistakes.</p>

